WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.837 --> 00:00:04.517
This is the hydraulic power unit of an injection moulding machine,

2
00:00:04.715 --> 00:00:07.826
a machine that shoots molten plastic into a mould.

3
00:00:08.597 --> 00:00:12.429
On the oil tank stand a pump, a coupling and an electric motor.

4
00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:15.357
Who looks after this machine every day?

5
00:00:16.345 --> 00:00:20.443
TPM, total productive maintenance, has its own answer to that.

6
00:00:21.257 --> 00:00:24.160
It came from Japan in the nineteen seventies.

7
00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:29.432
The goal is zero breakdowns, zero defects and zero accidents.

8
00:00:30.474 --> 00:00:33.760
That only works when equipment care is everyone’s job,

9
00:00:33.899 --> 00:00:36.209
not only the maintenance department’s.

10
00:00:36.908 --> 00:00:41.645
So also the job of the people who run the machine every day: the operators.

11
00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:45.841
Most plants build TPM on eight pillars.

12
00:00:46.597 --> 00:00:48.292
They include planned maintenance,

13
00:00:48.385 --> 00:00:51.601
focused improvement, quality maintenance and training.

14
00:00:52.622 --> 00:00:55.478
The best-known pillar is autonomous maintenance.

15
00:00:56.169 --> 00:00:58.944
The operators own the basic care of their machine.

16
00:00:59.705 --> 00:01:04.523
They clean, inspect, lubricate and tighten, and they report what looks wrong.

17
00:01:05.582 --> 00:01:07.707
Planned maintenance is the other half.

18
00:01:08.504 --> 00:01:11.163
With the routine work moved to the operators,

19
00:01:11.325 --> 00:01:14.843
technicians have time for skilled work: condition monitoring,

20
00:01:15.017 --> 00:01:18.442
and finding the root cause of failures that keep coming back.

21
00:01:19.463 --> 00:01:23.770
A typical split: operators do 5 to 10 minutes of checks per shift,

22
00:01:23.933 --> 00:01:26.162
and technicians fix what they find.

23
00:01:27.476 --> 00:01:31.145
Autonomous maintenance is usually rolled out in seven steps,

24
00:01:31.307 --> 00:01:33.246
and the first is an initial clean.

25
00:01:33.934 --> 00:01:37.870
With the machine safely off, the team cleans it from top to bottom,

26
00:01:37.974 --> 00:01:41.143
including the places nobody has reached for years.

27
00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:43.905
In this pilot it takes one shift.

28
00:01:44.939 --> 00:01:46.413
Cleaning is inspection.

29
00:01:47.118 --> 00:01:50.532
Clean with attention, and you see what the dirt was hiding.

30
00:01:51.234 --> 00:01:53.916
Here the pump is leaking oil, drop by drop.

31
00:01:54.934 --> 00:01:57.035
On the motor foot a bolt is loose.

32
00:01:57.771 --> 00:01:59.396
The coupling guard has a crack.

33
00:02:00.129 --> 00:02:03.670
On a dirty machine you only see that when something goes wrong.

34
00:02:04.991 --> 00:02:09.472
Every abnormality the team finds gets a tag: a coloured card on the machine.

35
00:02:10.169 --> 00:02:12.595
It says what is wrong and who fixes it.

36
00:02:13.663 --> 00:02:17.494
After one shift, 37 tags hang on this one machine.

37
00:02:18.567 --> 00:02:23.873
They are 12 oil leaks, 9 loose bolts, 6 missing guards and 10 other faults.

38
00:02:24.653 --> 00:02:28.217
Left alone, many of them grow into a breakdown later.

39
00:02:29.524 --> 00:02:35.387
Operators fix 21 themselves in the first week: tightening a bolt, securing a clamp,

40
00:02:35.585 --> 00:02:36.874
clearing a dirt trap.

41
00:02:37.898 --> 00:02:42.706
The other 16 go to maintenance, with a clear promise: closed within 4 weeks.

42
00:02:43.711 --> 00:02:45.511
This is what makes or breaks it.

43
00:02:46.283 --> 00:02:51.519
When tags hang for months, operators stop reporting, because nothing happens anyway.

44
00:02:52.267 --> 00:02:54.334
Then autonomous maintenance dies.

45
00:02:55.356 --> 00:03:00.174
So here is a sharp question for any plant: how many tags were hung last quarter,

46
00:03:00.371 --> 00:03:02.008
and what share of them is closed?

47
00:03:03.371 --> 00:03:06.889
After the clean comes the daily routine: CILT.

48
00:03:07.604 --> 00:03:11.482
The letters stand for clean, inspect, lubricate and tighten.

49
00:03:12.501 --> 00:03:16.959
Clean and inspect: the operator wipes the machine down and looks for leaks,

50
00:03:17.087 --> 00:03:18.561
cracks and loose parts.

51
00:03:19.591 --> 00:03:24.039
Lubricate: grease on the bearing, in the right place and the right amount.

52
00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:25.221
Tighten:

53
00:03:25.465 --> 00:03:28.425
a match mark painted across bolt and nut shows

54
00:03:28.518 --> 00:03:31.061
at a glance whether a bolt has worked loose.

55
00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:36.831
Good checks are visual: min and max marks on the oil glass, a coloured zone,

56
00:03:36.970 --> 00:03:39.025
often green, on the pressure gauge.

57
00:03:39.808 --> 00:03:42.734
Then anyone sees at once whether it is right.

58
00:03:43.797 --> 00:03:46.874
On this machine it is a checklist of 8 points.

59
00:03:47.598 --> 00:03:50.733
It takes 7 minutes at the start of every shift.

60
00:03:52.067 --> 00:03:55.875
After three months, breakdowns drop from 9 to 4 a month.

61
00:03:56.890 --> 00:04:00.535
If each breakdown costs 2.5 hours on average,

62
00:04:00.756 --> 00:04:07.281
that is five breakdowns times 2.5 hours: 12.5 hours of downtime saved a month.

63
00:04:08.294 --> 00:04:12.648
And hydraulic oil top-ups fall from 60 to 15 litres a month.

64
00:04:13.342 --> 00:04:16.489
The leaks are fixed, so the oil stays in the machine.

65
00:04:17.273 --> 00:04:20.675
Many plants stall exactly there, at step two or three,

66
00:04:20.815 --> 00:04:24.286
when the sources of dirt and leaks must actually be fixed.

67
00:04:25.348 --> 00:04:27.960
That only works with maintenance on board.

68
00:04:28.665 --> 00:04:33.088
Technicians train the operators and decide which checks go on the list.

69
00:04:33.849 --> 00:04:35.753
Everything beyond it stays with them.

70
00:04:37.057 --> 00:04:40.122
You measure the progress of TPM with OEE,

71
00:04:40.285 --> 00:04:45.417
overall equipment effectiveness: availability times performance times quality.

72
00:04:46.391 --> 00:04:48.992
Fewer breakdowns give higher availability.

73
00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:54.563
Fewer micro-stops, short stops of seconds to minutes, give higher performance.

74
00:04:55.369 --> 00:04:57.958
That is how the clean shows up in the numbers.

75
00:04:58.985 --> 00:05:03.873
85 percent is often quoted as the world-class benchmark, a rule of thumb.

76
00:05:04.653 --> 00:05:07.254
Measured honestly, many plants land far lower;

77
00:05:07.405 --> 00:05:11.945
the module Capacity and flow breaks that figure down into the six big losses.

78
00:05:12.966 --> 00:05:15.800
It is the trend that counts, not one number.

79
00:05:16.554 --> 00:05:18.249
A short round every shift,

80
00:05:18.505 --> 00:05:23.428
every tag closed fast: that is how caring for the machine becomes normal work.
